Demo 3 reveals the full rule. The two same-color edge segments (here horizontal: top cols 11–16, bottom cols 0–5) pair up by offset. Each stray marker lies within one segment's span and casts a ray toward that segment, turning into 4 (e.g., input 8s in the top segment's column span filled upward, stopping at the 9s). The paired cell in the opposite segment (same offset along the segment) casts a ray the other way, filling to the edge. That explains all three demos exactly, including the previously puzzling filled rows/columns.

Applying to the test: the 7s (rows 4,5) lie in the left segment's row span, so they cast leftward (becoming 4, filling cols 1–2 with 7). Their partners in the right segment are rows 0,1 (offsets 0,1), which fill leftward from col 5 to the edge. Rows 2 and 6 pair with each other but have no markers, so they stay unchanged.

